// TEST: ML Perm Write Index Rejected (rejects)
// ERROR: BL4012
import ml as ml
// ============================================================================
// THE WRITE PATH, brought level with the read path (stage 5c catalog, finding
// 2). MLGalilean's judgeAssign folded over an element write's index arguments
// and required every one of them boost-invariant. MLEquiv's and MLPerm's
// matched the write TARGET, checked the value, and walked straight past the
// index expressions — so the very same expression was rejected when read and
// unexamined when used to choose a cell to write.
//
//     let z = x(0)     // BL4012: component access into node-covariant 'x'
//     acc(x(0)) = 1.0  // accepted
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THE SECOND LINE IS NOT EQUIVARIANT
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `x` is Pow 1: extent 4 = 4^1 at N = 4. The classifier keys on the FLAT
// EXTENT of the `Idx<>` axis and says nothing about the element type, so an
// INTEGER node power is a node power — which is also what makes this program
// well-typed, since x(0) is then an Int and can index.
//
// `acc` has extent 3, not a power of 4, so it is Pow 0 — an invariant scratch
// buffer, and writing into it is fine in itself. What is not fine is writing
// into it AT A CELL CHOSEN BY x(0). Relabel the four nodes and x(0) is a
// different node's number, so the 1.0 lands in a different cell of acc and
// the readout acc(0) changes. The function claims S_4-equivariance; the
// location of its only write is a function of the labelling.
//
// v1 defers component READS of a node power uniformly (it has no
// loop-variable tracking, so it cannot tell a bound-index read that
// reassembles equivariantly from a fixed-offset one that does not — MLPerm.fs
// says so at length). The point of this test is only that the write path
// makes the SAME judgment about the SAME expression, and reports it at the
// index rather than at the assignment. BL4012.
// ============================================================================
function bad(x: Array<Int like Idx<4>>) where ml.perm_equiv(4) -> Float = {
    let mut acc: Array<Float like Idx<3>> = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]
    acc(x(0)) = 1.0
    acc(0)
}

let out = bad([2, 1, 0, 1])
